const a = { d: new Date(), m: new Map([[1, 'x']]), u: undefined };
JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(a)); // { d: '2026-...Z' }  -> Date becomes a string, Map becomes {}, u dropped
structuredClone(a);              // { d: Date, m: Map, u: undefined }
```

The JSON round-trip only keeps what JSON can represent: Dates turn into strings, Map/Set become empty objects, undefined and functions disappear, and circular references throw. structuredClone uses the browser/Node structured clone algorithm, so it keeps Dates, Maps, Sets, typed arrays, and handles cycles. It still can't copy functions, DOM nodes or class prototypes (instances come back as plain objects). It's also usually faster for big objects. Your reviewer is probably right to prefer structuredClone.

